::ENG1_U01_L05_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The school added a sign-in table for tutoring. Students wrote their names and subjects so tutors could see who needed help first. Question: Which sentence is written correctly?{
~A. the sign-in table helped tutors see who needed help first#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
=B. The sign-in table helped tutors see who needed help first.#Correct. This is a complete sentence with correct capitalization and punctuation.
~C. Because tutors could see who needed help first.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. The sign-in table helped tutors it showed names.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The park added map signs near the trail entrance. Visitors used the signs to choose a walking route. Question: Which sentence fixes the fragment "Because visitors used the signs to choose a route"?{
=A. The signs helped visitors because they used them to choose a route.#Correct. This is a complete sentence that keeps the meaning.
~B. Because visitors used the signs to choose a route.#Incorrect. This repeats the fragment.
~C. visitors used signs to choose a route#Incorrect. This lacks capitalization and punctuation.
~D. The signs visitors used because route.#Incorrect. This is not a clear sentence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The teacher posted the homework steps on the board. Students checked the steps before starting the assignment. Question: Which response uses correct punctuation?{
~A. The steps helped students, they checked them before starting.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~B. The steps helped students they checked them before starting.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=C. The steps helped students because they checked them before starting.#Correct. This sentence is punctuated correctly and connects the idea clearly.
~D. The steps helped students because they checked them before starting#Incorrect. This needs end punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The school posted lunch menus online. Families could review meal choices before school. Question: Which sentence uses capitalization correctly?{
~A. the online menu helped families review meal choices.#Incorrect. The first word should be capitalized.
=B. The online menu helped families review meal choices.#Correct. This sentence begins with a capital letter and is written correctly.
~C. the Online Menu helped Families review meal choices.#Incorrect. Common nouns in this sentence should not be randomly capitalized.
~D. The online menu helped families review meal choices#Incorrect. This has capitalization but needs end punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The media center created a quiet checkout line. Students who had already chosen books could leave more quickly. Question: Which response is a complete sentence?{
=A. The quiet checkout line helped ready students leave more quickly.#Correct. This is a complete sentence with clear meaning.
~B. Because students had already chosen books.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~C. A quiet checkout line near the media center.#Incorrect. This does not express a complete thought.
~D. students could leave more quickly#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q06::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The science class used a chart to record plant growth. Students compared the measurements each Friday. Question: Which sentence is written correctly?{
~A. The chart helped students, they compared measurements each Friday.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The chart helped students compare plant measurements each Friday.#Correct. This is a complete, correctly punctuated sentence.
~C. the chart helped students compare plant measurements each friday.#Incorrect. It needs capitalization for the first word and Friday.
~D. Because the chart helped students compare measurements.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q07::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The counselor sent deadline reminders to students. The reminders helped students submit forms on time. Question: Which revision fixes the run-on "The reminders helped students they submitted forms on time"?{
~A. The reminders helped students, they submitted forms on time.#Incorrect. This comma splice still joins two complete thoughts incorrectly.
=B. The reminders helped students because they submitted forms on time.#Correct. This revision connects the idea and reason clearly.
~C. Because reminders helped students submit forms on time.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~D. reminders helped students submit forms on time#Incorrect. This lacks capitalization and punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q08::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The debate team practiced with a timer. Speakers learned to finish claims and evidence within the limit. Question: Which response is written correctly?{
~A. the timer helped speakers finish claims and evidence within the limit.#Incorrect. The first word needs capitalization.
~B. The timer helped speakers finish claims and evidence within the limit#Incorrect. The sentence needs end punctuation.
=C. The timer helped speakers finish claims and evidence within the limit.#Correct. This is written correctly.
~D. The timer helped speakers, finish claims and evidence within the limit.#Incorrect. The comma incorrectly separates the verb from the rest of the sentence.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q09::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The art teacher labeled supply bins. Students returned markers and glue to the correct places. Question: Which sentence is clearest and correct?{
=A. The labeled bins helped students return supplies to the correct places.#Correct. This sentence is clear and follows standard English conventions.
~B. The labeled bins helped students, return supplies to the correct places.#Incorrect. The comma incorrectly interrupts the sentence.
~C. the labeled bins helped students return supplies to the correct places#Incorrect. This needs capitalization and punctuation.
~D. Because students returned markers and glue.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q10::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The city added brighter crosswalk paint. Drivers could see the crossing area more easily. Question: Which response avoids a run-on?{
~A. The brighter paint helped drivers they could see the crossing area more easily.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
=B. The brighter paint helped drivers see the crossing area more easily.#Correct. This is a complete sentence with correct sentence boundaries.
~C. The brighter paint helped drivers, they could see the crossing area more easily.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
~D. brighter paint helped drivers see crossing area#Incorrect. This needs capitalization, article use, and punctuation.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q11::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The teacher gave students sentence starters. Students used them to explain evidence more clearly. Question: Which sentence uses standard English correctly?{
=A. Sentence starters helped students because they explained evidence more clearly.#Correct. This is a complete and correctly punctuated sentence.
~B. sentence starters helped students because they explained evidence more clearly.#Incorrect. The first word needs capitalization.
~C. Sentence starters helped students because they explained evidence more clearly#Incorrect. This sentence needs end punctuation.
~D. Because sentence starters helped students explain evidence.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q12::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The library created a shelf for recommended books. Students found books their classmates enjoyed. Question: Which response is correctly written?{
~A. The shelf helped students, they found recommended books.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=B. The shelf helped students find recommended books.#Correct. This is clear and correctly punctuated.
~C. the shelf helped students find recommended books.#Incorrect. The first word needs capitalization.
~D. Because students found recommended books.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
}

::ENG1_U01_L05_Q13::[MLA.ENG1.L.01] Passage: The school website posted club times. Students checked the times before going to meetings. Question: Which sentence fixes the draft "students checked the website before meetings"?{
=A. Students checked the website before meetings.#Correct. The sentence now begins with a capital letter and ends with punctuation.
